WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.502
-Our first guests tonight
are two Hollywood legends

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.970
that also happen to be brothers.

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.872
Their new book, "The Boys: A
Memoir of Hollywood and Family"

00:00:06.872 --> 00:00:08.141
is available tomorrow.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:10.243
Please welcome to the show
Ron and Clint Howard.

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:11.411
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:21.421 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:29.062 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:30.096
-Thank you.

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:31.797
-Thank you for being here,
fellas.

00:00:31.797 --> 00:00:33.633
-Wow!
-So, "The Boys,"

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:35.668
this is not a -- this is
a nickname your mom had

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:37.270
not just for the two of you,
correct?

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.607
-All three of her boys.
-The two boys and her husband.

00:00:40.607 --> 00:00:42.809
She called you guys "the boys."
-So, it's a little tribute

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.476
to mom in addition
to all the other stories

00:00:44.476 --> 00:00:47.580
that focus a lot on dad.
But she --

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.950
without her, her energy,
her vision, her creativity,

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.785
none of it would have happened.
-And one of the things

00:00:52.785 --> 00:00:54.687
you guys talk about
is that your dad --

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.355
there was maybe a bit
of a mythology about

00:00:56.355 --> 00:00:57.624
how your parents met,

00:00:57.624 --> 00:01:00.126
that, over the years,
he became a little honest,

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:02.162
a little more forthcoming
about the truth.

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:04.296
-Well, he waited --
he waited till mom passed

00:01:04.296 --> 00:01:05.698
before he really
spilled the beans.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:06.766
-Okay.
-That is true.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.034
Well, there was a whole --

00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:10.436
Look, their whole love story
is kind of a fairy tale.

00:01:10.436 --> 00:01:12.472
It's one of the reasons
that we wanted to tell it.

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:14.673
These two people, they had --
you know, they, later in life

00:01:14.673 --> 00:01:16.843
called themselves
the sophisticated hicks.

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:19.145
They had no business
dreaming of Hollywood.

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.114
Dad wanted to be
a singing cowboy.

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:24.150
Couldn't carry a tune.
Thank God nobody told him.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.317
[ Laughter ]

00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:28.187
But they met at
University of Oklahoma,

00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:31.657
they fell in love,
and, you know, the reality is,

00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:33.326
they ran off to be
in the business together.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:35.294
They ran to New York,
this magical place,

00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:37.063
to try to do it,
and they actually succeeded.

00:01:37.063 --> 00:01:40.366
But the story was always,
"Yeah, you know,

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:42.935
I left, and I got
a summer-stock thing,

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:46.372
and she went to New York,
and then, lo and behold, we --

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:48.874
we discovered
each other there again,

00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:51.077
and then we --
we somehow got cast

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:53.146
in this children's theater
thing together.

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:56.415
We both got jobs!
Wow! And then we just, you know,

00:01:56.415 --> 00:01:58.718
we fell in love there,
and eventually we got married

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:01.353
in Kentucky because
they had no waiting period."

00:02:01.353 --> 00:02:02.855
This whole long story.

00:02:02.855 --> 00:02:05.024
They ran off.
They shacked up.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.258
[ Laughter ]
I mean, come on.

00:02:06.258 --> 00:02:08.827
And dad finally,
when mom passed, he said,

00:02:08.827 --> 00:02:10.395
"You know..."
What am I?

00:02:10.395 --> 00:02:12.865
Probably 48 years old.

00:02:12.865 --> 00:02:15.267
"You know, that story
we told you --"

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:17.370
"Dad, dad! Just --
You don't have to go there.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.537
We got it.
We got it."

00:02:18.537 --> 00:02:20.173
-But this is --
so you guys, obviously,

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:21.974
got started very young.
So young, Clint,

00:02:21.974 --> 00:02:24.811
that you do not remember
your first job on television.

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:28.214
-Oh, no. Not at all.
In fact, it was probably --

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:29.715
Well, I've been
in the business 60 years.

00:02:29.715 --> 00:02:31.918
-Yeah.
-I've been paying attention

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:33.586
for 55 years.
-Okay.

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:35.354
[ Laughter ]

00:02:35.354 --> 00:02:38.524
-I sort of had a blackout there
for the first five years.

00:02:38.524 --> 00:02:40.392
-I do want to show
this amazing photo.

00:02:40.392 --> 00:02:42.628
You were in an episode of --
I think you were 7 years old

00:02:42.628 --> 00:02:43.629
when you were in an episode
of "Star Trek."

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:45.798
-Yes.
-The original "Star Trek."

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:46.732
And what's the name
of your character?

00:02:46.732 --> 00:02:47.967
Do you remember that?
-Oh, yes.

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:49.268
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:52.371
I was Commander Balok
in "The Corbomite Maneuver."

00:02:52.371 --> 00:02:54.240
[ Laughter ]
Yeah, yeah.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.942
And as you can tell --
as you can tell, I wasn't having

00:02:56.942 --> 00:02:58.410
a very happy day there

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.079
when I was getting
the skull cap put on.

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:03.282
But it was better than the first
alternative that they gave me.

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:05.417
They really wanted
to shave my head.

00:03:05.417 --> 00:03:07.920
And as a kid going
to second grade, I thought,

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.855
"There is no way
I'm gonna go back to school

00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:11.757
with a bald head."
-Yeah.

00:03:11.757 --> 00:03:13.225
I mean, even being
on "Star Trek"

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:15.260
couldn't make up for that.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.596
But your dad, he had a sense --
You know, obviously,

00:03:17.596 --> 00:03:19.933
he had done -- you know,
he was in show business,

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:21.434
and he had kids
who were gonna be in it,

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:22.835
and he wanted you guys
to have normal lives,

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:24.737
but he also
wanted you to thrive.

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:27.173
And so tell the story
about how he would fake

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:29.341
a boom mic just so that
you would be used to it

00:03:29.341 --> 00:03:34.113
if you were ever on set?
-Yeah. Well, I mean, he --

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:38.518
My very first audition,
I was still only 3 years old.

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:40.887
And, you know,
we learned the scene.

00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:42.288
Turned out,
he was incredibly gifted

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:45.624
at basically teaching kids
how to understand how to act,

00:03:45.624 --> 00:03:47.393
not perform
but really understand

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:48.828
what was going on in a scene.

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:51.130
So I had memorized the scene,
I worked it out.

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:53.432
And it was for a movie called
"The Journey."

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:56.135
And he realized that when you --
I got for my screen test,

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:57.503
there were gonna be
a lot of distractions.

00:03:57.503 --> 00:04:00.339
So he got an out-of-work
acting buddy to come over,

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:03.542
and they hung a bucket
from a string, and he would,

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:05.178
like, move it around.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:05.178 --> 00:04:06.979
-And, "Oh, no,
don't look up at that.

00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:10.049
Just look at the other actor."
My mom had a cereal box,

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:11.984
pretending to be a camera
that was kind of moving around.

00:04:11.984 --> 00:04:13.219
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:14.553
And so, it was that kind of --

00:04:14.553 --> 00:04:16.456
A little like Tiger Woods' dad
yelling in the backswing.

00:04:16.456 --> 00:04:18.324
-Yeah.
-But much kinder.

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:20.159
-Well, I will tell you this.
I have a 3-year-old,

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:22.294
and if there was a bucket
in the room, forget it.

00:04:22.294 --> 00:04:24.163
[ Laughter ]
You know.

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:25.431
If there was a bucket
within eyesight,

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.133
he'd be like "What?"

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:30.403
-Mom and dad understood the
distractions of the business.

00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:32.905
-Yeah.
-Both the physical distractions

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:34.940
and then
the psychological distractions.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.643
And they were really good
at heading those off.

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:39.545
You know?
They always --

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:41.814
Boy, they gave us
so much confidence.

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:43.549
Mom and dad gave us
so much confidence.

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:45.951
It was just -- you know, it was
a beautiful life growing up.

00:04:45.951 --> 00:04:47.487
And also,
it was a beautiful life

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:50.123
being this guy's little brother.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:52.258
-But now --
That's very nice.

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:55.727
But you also had to negotiate
a little, Clint, because you,

00:04:55.727 --> 00:04:56.929
at a very young age, obviously,

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:58.998
you knew that you wanted
to be behind the camera.

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:01.567
-Yeah.
-You made some short films.

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.269
And you were very kind
to cast Clint,

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:04.704
but you wanted to make sure
that there was

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:06.139
a little something in it
for you, right?

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:08.574
-Well, I was getting more and
more serious about making films,

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:11.110
and I wanted
to enter a contest --

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:13.612
The Kodak
Teenage Filmmakers Contest.

00:05:13.612 --> 00:05:16.215
And they had a category called
"The One Reeler,"

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:17.983
where you had to plan out
all of your shots

00:05:17.983 --> 00:05:19.118
and you couldn't edit it.

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:20.986
You just had to shoot it
and send it in.

00:05:20.986 --> 00:05:24.490
And I built the whole thing
around this young kid.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.724
And it was kind of
a "Twilight Zone"-ish

00:05:25.724 --> 00:05:26.893
sort of thing.

00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:28.694
There was a part for Clint,
part for dad.

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:31.296
And we're getting ready
to do it.

00:05:31.296 --> 00:05:33.732
Something I carefully planned.
I'm very nervous about it.

00:05:33.732 --> 00:05:36.569
And I asked Clint to do it.
He sort of said yeah.

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:38.904
We get there.
We're getting ready to shoot,

00:05:38.904 --> 00:05:42.875
and he says, "I -- I hear
there's a cash prize involved

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:44.777
if you win something."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:47.713
I said, "Yeah. You can --
You know, first is $200.

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:49.915
Second's, you know, $150."

00:05:49.915 --> 00:05:54.253
And he said, "Well,
I'm gonna need half of that."

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:56.622
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:59.157
-And I told him, I said,
"Hey, listen, bud.

00:05:59.157 --> 00:06:01.994
If you want to scour
the neighborhood in Toluca Lake

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:04.697
and see if you can find another
12-year-old that wants to be

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:08.634
in your short, go right ahead."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:11.136
-And you won.
-I -- I came in second.

00:06:11.136 --> 00:06:12.738
-So, did he show up
for the money?

00:06:12.738 --> 00:06:13.939
-Hell yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.939 --> 00:06:16.843
I mean, the minute --
I'm there, beaming --

00:06:16.843 --> 00:06:19.879
"I got a -- I came in second!
And he's...

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:21.914
[ Laughter ]

00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:23.315
-We had dad mediate.

00:06:23.315 --> 00:06:27.986
Dad came in and immediately
mediated, and I prevailed.

00:06:27.986 --> 00:06:31.624
And I got the $75.
-I mean, it must've --

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:33.426
You guys also talk about the
fact that you were obviously,

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:35.994
you know, you were in shows
that were incredibly popular.

00:06:35.994 --> 00:06:37.997
You know, "Andy Griffith,"
and you were in "Gentle Ben,"

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:40.332
and people knew who you were.
And I always kind of wondered

00:06:40.332 --> 00:06:42.034
about something like this,
because again, you went back,

00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:43.402
and you tried
to have a normal life.

00:06:43.402 --> 00:06:45.037
You played basketball.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:46.205
-But you're playing basketball,

00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:48.340
and every single kid
knows who you are.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.776
Right? Hold on.
I have a great photo.

00:06:50.776 --> 00:06:52.511
There you are there.
-Yeah.

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:53.679
[ Laughter ]

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:56.882
-And I guess
it made it pretty easy

00:06:56.882 --> 00:06:58.784
for the other team
to try to get in your head.

00:06:58.784 --> 00:07:00.386
-There was always a lot of that.

00:07:00.386 --> 00:07:02.421
There was a lot of that
at school.

00:07:02.421 --> 00:07:05.291
You know?
And it was --

00:07:05.291 --> 00:07:07.626
You know, it was something
I had to learn to cope with,

00:07:07.626 --> 00:07:10.395
whether that meant
I had to get in a fight,

00:07:10.395 --> 00:07:12.197
a wrestling match or something.

00:07:12.197 --> 00:07:14.167
Later, when I got
into high school,

00:07:14.167 --> 00:07:17.169
and we're playing at away games,

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:19.872
you know,
they rode me pretty hard.

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:22.308
And at some schools,
they did this thing

00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:24.410
where I'd be
at the free-throw line,

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.411
having to take
a couple of foul shots,

00:07:26.411 --> 00:07:28.180
and all of a sudden,
you'd hear the band,

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:29.448
and the band would go...

00:07:29.448 --> 00:07:32.985
[ Singing to the tune of
"The Andy Griffith Show" theme ]

00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:36.222
And then, "Miss it, Opie!"
[ Laughter ]

00:07:36.222 --> 00:07:38.457
And I'm here, and --
But I am proud to say

00:07:38.457 --> 00:07:41.060
that I was
a 60% free-throw shooter at home

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:42.961
where the crowd was friendly.
-Yeah.

00:07:42.961 --> 00:07:44.730
-87% on the road.
-There you go.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:45.898
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:49.201
There you go.

00:07:49.201 --> 00:07:50.803
I want to show this photo
because I feel like I've seen

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:52.905
so many photos
of the "Happy Days"

00:07:52.905 --> 00:07:55.174
softball team over the years.
-Oh, it was a blast.

00:07:55.174 --> 00:07:57.009
-And here you are
with Henry Winkler,

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.778
who, I think we can
all attest -- bad guy.

00:07:58.778 --> 00:08:01.614
Just a deeply...
-Miserable S.O.B.

00:08:01.614 --> 00:08:03.015
-Miserable human being!

00:08:03.015 --> 00:08:04.984
-Literally the nicest person
I've ever met.

00:08:04.984 --> 00:08:06.285
-Godfather to all my kids,
by the way.

00:08:06.285 --> 00:08:08.253
-Really?
Well, you've chosen well.

00:08:08.253 --> 00:08:09.922
Clint, true story --
you taught him to pitch?

00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:11.990
-Yes, I did.
I played high school baseball,

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:13.592
and I had
some pretty good chops

00:08:13.592 --> 00:08:15.261
and had the opportunity
to play on

00:08:15.261 --> 00:08:16.962
the "Happy Days" softball team.
-He was sort of a ringer.

00:08:16.962 --> 00:08:18.630
We grabbed him onto the team.

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:22.568
-And Henry had no skill at all
as a baseball player,

00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:24.337
and yet within
a few short weeks,

00:08:24.337 --> 00:08:26.505
he had learned to develop
this wonderful pitch

00:08:26.505 --> 00:08:28.674
that sort of drifted inside.

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:30.776
And he could tie up
really good hitters.

00:08:30.776 --> 00:08:32.878
-Well, much like your father,
you were a good teacher.

00:08:32.878 --> 00:08:35.448
-Yes, yeah.
Softball pitcher whisperer.

00:08:35.448 --> 00:08:37.450
[ Laughter ]
-We all need one.

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:40.052
Gentlemen, congrats on the book.
It's such a lovely read.

00:08:40.052 --> 00:08:42.221
And thanks so much for making
the time for us tonight.

